The package now imports cleanly and functions execute correctly (it was previously broken due to the unbuildable `_KernSmooth` extension import).

**Summary of what was done:**
- Source: 16 JSON files in `.../5/all.R/` (a directory despite the `.R` name), each holding one converted R→Python function.
- Deduplicated/sorted imports across all files → `math`, `typing.Any`, `warnings` (stdlib), then `numpy`, `scipy.stats.beta` (third-party).
- Dropped all `from .xxx import yyy` local imports — every one referenced another function from this same set, so they became simple same-file references once combined (no import needed).
- Dropped the pre-existing `from . import _KernSmooth` line: none of the 16 functions call the compiled Fortran extension (they're pure NumPy/SciPy reimplementations), and that import was actually broken since `_KernSmooth` isn't built in this tree — it was crashing every `import r2py_kernsmooth`.
- Kept the original `__all__` list unchanged (14 public functions; `onAttach`/`onUnload` stay defined but unexported, matching R's private `.onAttach`/`.onUnload` convention).
- Wrote all 16 functions alphabetically into `r2py_kernsmooth/__init__.py`, verified via `py_compile`, AST inspection, and a live import + smoke test (`bkde`, `linbin`).
